   {"id":"ap1","type":"explain","title":"What pipelining does","content":"Pipelining divides instruction execution into stages so multiple instructions can be in different stages at the same time. It improves throughput by overlapping work; it does not necessarily reduce the latency of one instruction.","code":"Instruction 1: Fetch → Decode → Execute\nInstruction 2:          Fetch → Decode → Execute","output":"Stages overlap across instructions","explanation":"The pipeline can complete instructions more frequently once it is filled."},
   {"id":"ap2","type":"explain","title":"Pipeline hazards","content":"A structural hazard occurs when hardware resources conflict. A data hazard occurs when an instruction depends on a result not yet available. A control hazard occurs when branches change which instruction should execute next. Processors use techniques such as stalls, forwarding, and branch prediction to manage hazards.","code":"Instruction 1: R1 = R2 + R3\nInstruction 2: R4 = R1 + R5  // depends on R1","output":"The second instruction depends on the first instruction's result","explanation":"The dependency may require forwarding or a stall, depending on the processor pipeline."},
